Packaging notes for pi-coding-agent
===================================

Build requirements
------------------

The package builds and runs with Debian trixie's Node.js 20 and npm 9.
Network access to the npm registry is required for npm ci and production
closure resolution. Model compilation uses committed data and does not
contact live catalogs. This is not a network-isolated Debian build.

Node.js 20 compatibility
-----------------------

Upstream targets Node.js >= 22.19. The Debian package requires >= 20.18.1:

  * The production closure pins undici to 7.29.1. Upstream's undici 8.x
    needs Node 22.19, while 7.29.1 supports Node 20.18.1 and the HTTP APIs
    used by pi. Compilation still uses upstream's development dependencies.

  * pi-ai compiles the committed src/providers/data snapshot with tsc and
    copies the data into dist verbatim. The snapshot comes from the
    @earendil-works/pi-ai@1.0.4 npm tarball, including the Meta and Radius
    catalogs. Catalog updates are a maintainer operation, not a Debian
    build step.

  * coding-agent uses build:unbundled. The upstream bundle validator rejects
    undici 8.x's node:sqlite import on Node 20. The Debian launcher and
    package metadata both reference the unbundled entrypoints.

  * The package-manager patch replaces fs.globSync, which needs Node 22,
    with readdirSync and minimatch for resource discovery.

  * Chord's plugin bundler targets Node 20.18. The runtime closure substitutes
    esbuild-wasm 0.28.2 for esbuild 0.28.2, preserving plugin bundling without
    embedding architecture-specific executables. WASM bundling can be slower
    than the native implementation. Neither dependency installation runs
    lifecycle scripts.

The native source format does not apply quilt patches automatically.
debian/apply-patches applies compatibility patches before compilation and
reverses them during clean. It accepts an already-applied or already-reversed
patch only after checking the complete opposite patch state. Unknown or
partially applied source states fail the build rather than skipping patches.

How the package is assembled
----------------------------

  1. Install development dependencies with npm ci --ignore-scripts.

  2. Build and pack eight local workspaces: chord, pi-telemetry, pi-tui,
     pi-ai, pi-agent-core, pi-codemode, pi-mcp, and pi-coding-agent. Upstream
     excludes the experimental remote harness from distributions; pi-client,
     pi-protocol, and pi-server are development-only dependencies.

  3. Resolve a production-only dependency tree into debian/install-root.
     Local tarballs are direct dependencies, not file-based overrides,
     which npm 9 rejects. undici uses a plain version override; Chord's
     esbuild dependency uses an npm alias directly in its package metadata.
     Upstream no longer ships npm-shrinkwrap.json. Transitive dependencies
     resolve from the registry at build time; the generated package-lock.json
     in debian/install-root records that build's resolution. Upstream's
     protobufjs override is applied at the install root.

  4. Install the application and dependency closure under /usr/lib/pi.
     /usr/bin/pi launches dist/cli.js using the system Node.js runtime.

The package omits optional native dependencies and the TUI's native helpers,
keeping Architecture: all. Clipboard text and image access use wl-clipboard
on Wayland or xclip on X11. These packages are suggested, not required for
headless use. Remote and headless text copy can use the terminal's OSC 52
clipboard support.

Source format
-------------

The package uses 3.0 (native); packaging lives inside the upstream source tree.
